# Sources other than Wikipedia for the Black Death story. Run: ./node_modules/.bin/harita sources black-death
# An address the command cannot fetch can be saved from a browser under the name the command prints.

# 090-aftermath, the-dead
https://www.persee.fr/doc/bec_0373-6237_1939_num_100_1_449196  # Gras 1939, the Givry register, its first page only
# not fetched: https://www.persee.fr/docAsPDF/bec_0373-6237_1939_num_100_1_449196.pdf  Gras 1939, the whole article, the count of 1348 on p. 307
# not fetched: https://www.clinicalmicrobiologyandinfection.org/article/S1198-743X(14)61625-6/fulltext  crisis burials of plague, CMI 2014, Givry 619 dead from 5 August to 19 November 1348
https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0008401  # Welford and Bossak 2010, Givry daily deaths
https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/6B70B323BC04D13C7EBCDBB991B728CB/S0025727300072100a.pdf/4_epidemiology_of_the_black_death_and_successive_waves_of_plague.pdf  # Cohn 2008, epidemiology of the Black Death, Givry, Florence, plagues of children
https://brema.suub.uni-bremen.de/download/pdf/2624908  # Schwarz 1996, Die Pest in Bremen, the entry of 1350 in the book of citizens
https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/med/marchione.asp  # Stefani, Florentine Chronicle, rubrics 643 and 635, the dead of Florence
https://archaeologydataservice.ac.uk/catalogue/adsdata/arch-1132-1/dissemination/pdf/068/068_300_360.pdf  # Thompson 1911, the registers of Bishop Gynewell of Lincoln, deaths of clergy 1349
https://islamiclaw.blog/2020/05/29/stuart-borsch/  # Borsch 2020, the Black Death in the Middle East, mortality of Egypt and Syria

